WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.000
If you lost track of Lou Diamond&nbsp;
Phillips after Longmire ended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.160
we've tallied up some of the best ways to&nbsp;
catch up with him. Here's what Phillips has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.760
been up to since the acclaimed Wyoming-set&nbsp;
crime drama aired its final episode in 2017.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.880
Even when Lou Diamond Phillips didn't&nbsp;
have a regular TV series, he was still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.880
a familiar face on TV, turning up regularly in&nbsp;
prominent guest star spots in just about every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:31.120
genre. This hasn't changed since Longmire wrapped.&nbsp;
Phillips has turned up on crime procedurals like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:37.840
Criminal Minds, and NCIS: New Orleans, as well&nbsp;
as landed a juicy recurring role on Blue Bloods.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:43.520
And, notably, it's only a recurring role because&nbsp;
of Phillips — he was too good for the show to let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.440
him go after only one episode. As Blue Bloods star&nbsp;
Donnie Wahlberg explained in an interview with Pop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.120
Culture, Phillips' character Louis Delgado was&nbsp;
originally slated to die in his first episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.720
But Wahlberg loved working with Phillips too&nbsp;
much for that to happen. He told Pop Culture,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:06.320
"It's just rare you get to work with somebody&nbsp;
you click with so well and they're amazing at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.920
what they do. I feel like he makes me a better&nbsp;
actor and he makes our show a better show."

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.040
Phillips' part on Blue Bloods&nbsp;
was accordingly expanded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.840
and his character wound up having a rich,&nbsp;
complex connection with Wahlberg's Danny Reagan.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:27.200
Lou Diamond Phillips has a distinctive voice, and&nbsp;
lately he's been putting it to good use. Since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.360
Longmire finale in 2017, Phillips has more than&nbsp;
doubled the amount of voice acting on his resume.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.760
On Disney's The Lion Guard, a Lion King spinoff&nbsp;
series, he provided the voice for Surak; he also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.600
had a recurring role on the children's show Elena&nbsp;
of Avalor and did a guest spot on Family Guy.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.480
This is an intriguing new direction for Phillips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.400
and it's one that makes a lot of&nbsp;
sense. He's always worked hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:58.480
make sure his career was as multifaceted as&nbsp;
possible. In an interview with UPI, he said,

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.600
"I literally trace [this versatility] back to&nbsp;
my theater days, which started in high school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.960
and then with my degree from the University&nbsp;
of Texas in Arlington. I did everything."

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.640
Notably, voice acting is also an easy&nbsp;
way for him to make appearances on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.120
children's shows like Elena of Avalor.&nbsp;
He told Yahoo Finance that, as a father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.840
he really appreciates the effort to provide&nbsp;
more diverse children's programming.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:28.640
After Longmire, if you wanted your regular Lou&nbsp;
Diamond Phillips fix, your best bet was the 2019&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.400
crime show Prodigal Son, where he played NYPD&nbsp;
Lieutenant Gil Arroyo. Warm and level-headed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.080
Arroyo is a kind of natural successor to&nbsp;
Henry Standing Bear. Phillips told UPI,

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.640
"The integrity and the moral compass&nbsp;
of Gil Arroyo is very much like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.480
of Henry [...] but one is a very&nbsp;
rural guy, a Native American,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.680
and the other one is an urban Filipino,&nbsp;
so it's nice to have that latitude."

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.160
The role is similar enough that Longmire&nbsp;
fans should check the show out, but still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.800
different enough to be a change of pace for&nbsp;
Phillips. He likes to keep things fresh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:08.240
Prodigal Son is a dark crime drama about&nbsp;
an investigator father was a serial killer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:14.240
The cases of the week can delve into some extreme&nbsp;
territory, but cast chemistry and sympathetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.520
characters keep it grounded. While the show was&nbsp;
unfortunately canceled after its second season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.800
two seasons of Gil Arroyo are&nbsp;
definitely better than none.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.360
While Lou Diamond Phillips actually&nbsp;
did a little directing before Longmire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.280
the series opened the door for him to do even&nbsp;
more. When he directed Season 6's "Fever," he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:39.120
clearly caught the directing bug. It's not that&nbsp;
unusual for actors to get the chance to direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.240
an episode on their own shows, but Phillips&nbsp;
really took the ball and ran with it. He didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:49.280
just take the reins on Longmire and Prodigal&nbsp;
Son, where he was a series regular — he also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.600
directed an episode of Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.,&nbsp;
as well as an episode of Fear the Walking Dead.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.120
"I feel very honored that they've given&nbsp;
me and trusted me an opportunity to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.880
you know, tell this story."

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.880
It's probably easier, he admitted in&nbsp;
an interview with Nerds and Beyond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.200
to keep his directing and acting&nbsp;
separate. Phillips told the outlet:

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.000
"I've often gotten so caught up in watching&nbsp;
my other actors during a scene that I forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.360
my own lines. That's why we have multiple takes!"

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.440
We can also look forward to seeing more of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.160
side of Phillips' career.&nbsp;
The actor went on to add:

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:28.000
"I absolutely love directing and consider it&nbsp;
to be simply yet another branch of my creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.120
tree. [...] I truly enjoy working with the other&nbsp;
artists (cinematographers, production designers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.240
stunt coordinators, every crew member really)&nbsp;
to take great ideas and actualize them."

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.000
Longmire fans who miss Lou Diamond&nbsp;
Phillips can also look for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.080
in a surprising place: the&nbsp;
bookstore. In October 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.120
he published the young adult science fiction&nbsp;
novel The Tinderbox: Soldier of Indira.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.000
Phillips told Nerds and Beyond that the story&nbsp;
stemmed from the manga-style concept art his wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.040
had done for a prospective graphic novel. Phillips&nbsp;
started the project off as a screenplay and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:06.320
"reverse-engineered" the story into a&nbsp;
novel, a project that took 10 years. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.880
told UPI that 2020 lockdowns gave him the&nbsp;
time he needed to buckle down and finish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.920
And the results are impressive. The&nbsp;
Nerd Daily reviewed the book, saying,

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.760
"Phillips has skillfully woven&nbsp;
together a fantastical story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.800
with realistic touchstones. [....] The plot&nbsp;
is driving, the characters are memorable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.880
and the writing is satisfying.&nbsp;
Enhanced by the striking drawings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.840
Phillips has built a world here which readers&nbsp;
will undoubtedly wish to visit again."

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.200
They might be in luck. Phillips confirmed with&nbsp;
UPI that a second book is already in the works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.080
With some novel-writing&nbsp;
experience now under his belt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:45.840
we're betting readers won't have&nbsp;
to wait 10 years for this next one.

